  </worksheets>
  <dashboards>
    <dashboard name='Notes'>
      <repository-location derived-from='https://tableau.deliveryhero.net/t/GlobalStandardReporting/workbooks/DPSABTestSignificanceDashboard/Notes?rev=' id='Notes' path='/t/GlobalStandardReporting/workbooks/MockupDPSABTestSignificanceDashboard' revision='' site='GlobalStandardReporting' />
      <style>
        <style-rule element='dash-text'>
          <format attr='vertical-align' id='dash-text_3' value='top' />
        </style-rule>
      </style>
      <size maxheight='3200' maxwidth='1000' minheight='3200' minwidth='1000' sizing-mode='fixed' />
      <zones>
        <zone h='100000' id='4' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
          <zone forceUpdate='true' h='95375' id='3' type-v2='text' w='98400' x='800' y='4375'>
            <formatted-text>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>Context</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The Significance Tab shows what p-values resulted from the A/B tests run by DPS. </run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>A p-value indicates whether the samples of a test differ significantly from one another.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>In the case of such a difference between samples, it is reasonable to consider changing schemes.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The p-value is compared to an error rate called alpha.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>Alpha is the risk of a type I error and is usually set at 5%.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>A type I error occurs when the null hypothesis is rejected even though it is actually true (false positive).</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The null hypothesis states that there is no difference between the groups in the population.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>Level of Observation</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The level of observation is different accross different KPIs.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>Some of the KPIs are on a transaction level, some KPIs are on a session level, and some are on the user or customer level.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>For example, when measuring the revenue, the basic level of information is the transaction, that means - we compare the average revenue </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>per transaction</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'> between the groups. However, when we measure the orders per user, the basic level of information is </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>the user</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'> - we compare the number of orders per user between the groups. Conversion rate is the fraction of sessions that ended in a successful transaction, so the level of observation is the </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>session</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>User/Customer Level</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: User CVR, Orders per User, Orders per Customer.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Session Level</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: CVR, mCVR2, mCVR3, mCVR4.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Order level</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Delivery Fee, Gross Food Value, Travel Time, Delivery Costs, Revenue, Profit.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>KPIs</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Order Level KPIs:</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Delivery Costs</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The total riders costs per order. Obtained by summing the weekly costs by city and repartitioning them per delivery based on the total time spent on that single delivery including the idle_time, then summing up the cost of all deliveries for order.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Delivery Fee</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The supposed-to-be-paid delivery fee of the order placed from the backend. This may differ from the actual delivery fee paid in the case of local marketing campaigns that are not signalled in central-dwh.orders.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>GFV</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Gross Food Value of the order placed from the backend</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Revenue</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The total income of an order. It’s the sum of the delivery fee (including VAT), commission and the vendor fee.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Profit</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The revenue of an order, after deducting the delivery costs.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Travel Time</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The time (min) it takes the rider to travel from vendor location coordinates to the customers. This KPI is only available for OD orders.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Delivery Distance</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The Manhattan distance (km) between the vendor location coordinates and customer location coordinates. This distance doesn&apos;t take into account potential stacked deliveries, and it&apos;s not the traveled distance. This KPI is only available for OD orders.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Fleet Delay</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Average lateness in minutes of an order at session start time. This KPI is only available for OD orders.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>User/Customer Level KPIs</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>:</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>User CVR</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Fraction of the users that ended up turning into customers. Calculated as the number of distinct customers divided by the number of distinct users.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>User CVR is different from CVR since User CVR shows how many of the users (as opposed to sessions in CVR) eventually turned into customers. As opposed to orders per user (see below), it does not take the number of orders into account but only the question if a user made at least one transaction or not. Therefore, for this KPI, there is no difference between a user that made 1 transaction and a user that made 5 transactions because they both are considered customers.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Orders per User</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Number of orders divided by number of distinct users.This is different from User CVR since it represents the </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>number</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'> of orders per user, whereas User CVR does not take the number of orders per user into account but only the question if the user made a transaction.The difference between Orders per User and Orders per Customer is that Orders per User also takes users who did not complete a transaction into account as users with 0 transactions.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Orders per Customer</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Number of orders divided by number of distinct customers. Similar to Orders per User, but does not take users who did not convert into account. A user that did not complete a transaction and therefore has 0 transactions is being taken into account in Orders per User but not in Orders per Customer.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Session Level</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>:</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Conversion Rate</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Fraction of sessions that ended in a successful transaction. Calculated as the number of distinct transactions divided by the number of distinct session_id’s.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>mcvr2:</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'> Fraction of sessions that ended in the menu page out of the sessions that visited the listing page.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>mcvr3:</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'> Fraction of sessions that ended in the checkout page out of the sessions that visited the menu page</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>mcvr4:</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'> Fraction of sessions that ended in a successful transaction out of the sessions that visited the checkout page.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>Values Counts</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The values counts that appear in the table represent the number of observations.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>For example: Values count for CVR is the number of sessions that are part of the test.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The values count for profit is the number of orders that have an existing profit datapoint.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>Data Robustness</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>For the result of the tests to be correct we must assure data robustness.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The major manipulations that happen before the tests run are:</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='10' underline='true'>Discarding variants with less than 30 observations</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='10'>:</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>For KPIs with normally distributed sample means, the threshold of observations per variant is 30. Metrics with less than 30 values for a certain variant will not be calculated.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Example 1</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Test X has 3 variants: Control, Variant1 and Variant2. We have 300 orders in the Control group, 300 orders in Variant1 group and 25 orders in Variant2. Variant2 will not be included in the analysis. The test that will be used is Welch&apos;s t-test , </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>and not </run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>Welch&apos;s ANOVA + Games Howell post-hoc test. This, because after discarding Variant2, we are left with 2 variants.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Example 2</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Test Y has 2 Variants: Control and Variant1. Each group has 35 orders. Alas! Due to a technical error, Variant2 does not have data for Delivery Fee for 10 orders (these 10 orders would represented with NA in the delivery_fee column in our tables). This means, that for Test Y, Variant1 only has 25 observations. This metric will not be calculated.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='10' underline='true'>Discarding incoherent variants from KPIs on a transaction level</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='10'>:</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The variant is set on a transaction level. Theoretically, a specific user should </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>not</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'> be assigned to more than one variant. However, this has happened in the past, that different log-ins or orders of a specific user or customer were assigned different variants. For the KPIs on a user/customer level, this would not make sense: each user/customer should have one constant variant assigned to her. If this is not the case, this user will be discarded from the analysis of the KPIs that work on a transaction level.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Example</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Sarah made 4 transactions during the test time. Her first 3 transactions received the value </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Control </run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>under column </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>variant</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>. Her 4th transaction received the value </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Variant1</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>. We cannot assign Sarah to any group, and therefore Sarah will not be included in the calculations of KPIs on a user/customer level.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>Choice of a statistical method</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>To determine the relevant statistical method for a specific testing setup, we first must answer the following questions :</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Are the samples independent from one another ?</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>Yes, per assumption.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>What is the distribution?</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>It depends on the KPI. </run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>Normal distribution: </run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Delivery Costs</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Delivery Distance</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Delivery Fee</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Fleet Delay</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- GFV</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Profit</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Revenue</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Travel Time</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Orders per Customer</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>Binomial distribution :</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Conversion Rate</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- mCVR2</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- mCVR3</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- mCVR4</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- User CVR</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Do the samples have equal variance ?</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>Per assumption - no.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>How many samples are we comparing with one another ?</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The answer varies from test to test. </run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The tests are divided in two groups : those with only 2 variants (regular A/B tests), and those with more than 2 variants (A/B/n tests).</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>Based on the answers to these questions, we can match our metrics with 3 different statistical methods :</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Chi square (binomial distribution with 2 variants)</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Chi square + Bonferroni correction (binomial distribution with &gt; 2 variants)</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Welch t-test (normal distribution with 2 variants)</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Welch ANOVA + Games Howell post-hoc test (normal distribution with &gt; 2 variants)</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Welch ANOVA + Games Howell - Two relevant P-Values</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The methodology of ANOVA with a post-hoc Games-Howell test requires looking at 2 p-values to decide if a result is significant.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>First, the ANOVA works on the test level (not between specific variants!) and asks - is there </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>any</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'> significant difference? </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Only</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'> if there is a significant result, the Games-Howell test can tell us where (i.e. between which variables) the significant difference is.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>Therefore, if a Games-Howell test shows a significant result between two variables but the ANOVA test produced an insignificant result for the test - the result is insignificant.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The results of the ANOVA tests can be found in Significance Levels (advanced). For most users, it is recommended to </run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>avoid</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'> using the advanced tab and base your analysis or the general tab.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>What is p-value?</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>P-Value is the probability of making a type I error.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>What is alpha ?</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>Alpha is the error rate allowed by the stakeholders. It is the upper threshold of acceptable p-value for the result to be considered statistically significant.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>What is the Bonferroni correction?</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000'>The Bonferroni correction is a method of dealing with the multiple comparisons problem by adjusting the p-value to represent the larger probability of type I error when performing multiple comparisons. To control for this larger probability, we multiply the p-value by the number of comparisons.</run>
            </formatted-text>
            <zone-style>
              <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
              <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
              <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
              <format attr='margin' value='4' />
            </zone-style>
          </zone>
          <zone forceUpdate='true' h='2844' id='5' type-v2='text' w='98400' x='800' y='250'>
            <formatted-text>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='20'>DPS A/B Test Dashboard</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#898989' fontsize='16'>Significance Levels - Methodology</run>
            </formatted-text>
            <zone-style>
              <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
              <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
              <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
              <format attr='margin' value='4' />
            </zone-style>
          </zone>
          <zone h='1281' id='221' name='Friendly Guide' show-title='false' w='98400' x='800' y='3094'>
            <zone-style>
              <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
              <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
              <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
              <format attr='margin' value='4' />
            </zone-style>
          </zone>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='margin' value='8' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
      </zones>
      <devicelayouts>
        <devicelayout auto-generated='true' name='Phone'>
          <size maxheight='700' minheight='700' sizing-mode='vscroll' />
          <zones>
            <zone h='100000' id='1263' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
              <zone h='84000' id='1262' param='vert' type-v2='layout-flow' w='84000' x='8000' y='8000'>
                <zone forceUpdate='true' h='2844' id='5' type-v2='text' w='98400' x='800' y='250'>
                  <formatted-text>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='20'>DPS A/B Test Dashboard</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#898989' fontsize='16'>Significance Levels - Methodology</run>
                  </formatted-text>
                  <zone-style>
                    <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                    <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                    <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                    <format attr='margin' value='4' />
                    <format attr='padding' value='0' />
                  </zone-style>
                </zone>
                <zone fixed-size='33' h='1281' id='221' is-fixed='true' name='Friendly Guide' show-title='false' w='98400' x='800' y='3094'>
                  <zone-style>
                    <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                    <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                    <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                    <format attr='margin' value='4' />
                    <format attr='padding' value='0' />
                  </zone-style>
                </zone>
                <zone forceUpdate='true' h='95375' id='3' type-v2='text' w='98400' x='800' y='4375'>
                  <formatted-text>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>Context</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The Significance Tab shows what p-values resulted from the A/B tests run by DPS. </run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>A p-value indicates whether the samples of a test differ significantly from one another.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>In the case of such a difference between samples, it is reasonable to consider changing schemes.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The p-value is compared to an error rate called alpha.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>Alpha is the risk of a type I error and is usually set at 5%.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>A type I error occurs when the null hypothesis is rejected even though it is actually true (false positive).</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The null hypothesis states that there is no difference between the groups in the population.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>Level of Observation</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The level of observation is different accross different KPIs.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>Some of the KPIs are on a transaction level, some KPIs are on a session level, and some are on the user or customer level.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>For example, when measuring the revenue, the basic level of information is the transaction, that means - we compare the average revenue </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>per transaction</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'> between the groups. However, when we measure the orders per user, the basic level of information is </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>the user</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'> - we compare the number of orders per user between the groups. Conversion rate is the fraction of sessions that ended in a successful transaction, so the level of observation is the </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>session</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>User/Customer Level</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: User CVR, Orders per User, Orders per Customer.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Session Level</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: CVR, mCVR2, mCVR3, mCVR4.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Order level</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Delivery Fee, Gross Food Value, Travel Time, Delivery Costs, Revenue, Profit.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>KPIs</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Order Level KPIs:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Delivery Costs</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The total riders costs per order. Obtained by summing the weekly costs by city and repartitioning them per delivery based on the total time spent on that single delivery including the idle_time, then summing up the cost of all deliveries for order.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Delivery Fee</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The supposed-to-be-paid delivery fee of the order placed from the backend. This may differ from the actual delivery fee paid in the case of local marketing campaigns that are not signalled in central-dwh.orders.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>GFV</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Gross Food Value of the order placed from the backend</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Revenue</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The total income of an order. It’s the sum of the delivery fee (including VAT), commission and the vendor fee.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Profit</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The revenue of an order, after deducting the delivery costs.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Travel Time</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The time (min) it takes the rider to travel from vendor location coordinates to the customers. This KPI is only available for OD orders.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Delivery Distance</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: The Manhattan distance (km) between the vendor location coordinates and customer location coordinates. This distance doesn&apos;t take into account potential stacked deliveries, and it&apos;s not the traveled distance. This KPI is only available for OD orders.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Fleet Delay</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Average lateness in minutes of an order at session start time. This KPI is only available for OD orders.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>User/Customer Level KPIs</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>User CVR</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Fraction of the users that ended up turning into customers. Calculated as the number of distinct customers divided by the number of distinct users.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>User CVR is different from CVR since User CVR shows how many of the users (as opposed to sessions in CVR) eventually turned into customers. As opposed to orders per user (see below), it does not take the number of orders into account but only the question if a user made at least one transaction or not. Therefore, for this KPI, there is no difference between a user that made 1 transaction and a user that made 5 transactions because they both are considered customers.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Orders per User</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Number of orders divided by number of distinct users.This is different from User CVR since it represents the </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>number</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'> of orders per user, whereas User CVR does not take the number of orders per user into account but only the question if the user made a transaction.The difference between Orders per User and Orders per Customer is that Orders per User also takes users who did not complete a transaction into account as users with 0 transactions.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Orders per Customer</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Number of orders divided by number of distinct customers. Similar to Orders per User, but does not take users who did not convert into account. A user that did not complete a transaction and therefore has 0 transactions is being taken into account in Orders per User but not in Orders per Customer.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Session Level</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Conversion Rate</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Fraction of sessions that ended in a successful transaction. Calculated as the number of distinct transactions divided by the number of distinct session_id’s.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>mcvr2:</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'> Fraction of sessions that ended in the menu page out of the sessions that visited the listing page.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>mcvr3:</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'> Fraction of sessions that ended in the checkout page out of the sessions that visited the menu page</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>mcvr4:</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'> Fraction of sessions that ended in a successful transaction out of the sessions that visited the checkout page.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>Values Counts</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The values counts that appear in the table represent the number of observations.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>For example: Values count for CVR is the number of sessions that are part of the test.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The values count for profit is the number of orders that have an existing profit datapoint.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>Data Robustness</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>For the result of the tests to be correct we must assure data robustness.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The major manipulations that happen before the tests run are:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='10' underline='true'>Discarding variants with less than 30 observations</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='10'>:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>For KPIs with normally distributed sample means, the threshold of observations per variant is 30. Metrics with less than 30 values for a certain variant will not be calculated.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Example 1</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Test X has 3 variants: Control, Variant1 and Variant2. We have 300 orders in the Control group, 300 orders in Variant1 group and 25 orders in Variant2. Variant2 will not be included in the analysis. The test that will be used is Welch&apos;s t-test , </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>and not </run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>Welch&apos;s ANOVA + Games Howell post-hoc test. This, because after discarding Variant2, we are left with 2 variants.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Example 2</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Test Y has 2 Variants: Control and Variant1. Each group has 35 orders. Alas! Due to a technical error, Variant2 does not have data for Delivery Fee for 10 orders (these 10 orders would represented with NA in the delivery_fee column in our tables). This means, that for Test Y, Variant1 only has 25 observations. This metric will not be calculated.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='10' underline='true'>Discarding incoherent variants from KPIs on a transaction level</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='10'>:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The variant is set on a transaction level. Theoretically, a specific user should </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>not</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'> be assigned to more than one variant. However, this has happened in the past, that different log-ins or orders of a specific user or customer were assigned different variants. For the KPIs on a user/customer level, this would not make sense: each user/customer should have one constant variant assigned to her. If this is not the case, this user will be discarded from the analysis of the KPIs that work on a transaction level.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Example</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>: Sarah made 4 transactions during the test time. Her first 3 transactions received the value </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Control </run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>under column </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>variant</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>. Her 4th transaction received the value </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Variant1</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>. We cannot assign Sarah to any group, and therefore Sarah will not be included in the calculations of KPIs on a user/customer level.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='12'>Choice of a statistical method</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>To determine the relevant statistical method for a specific testing setup, we first must answer the following questions :</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Are the samples independent from one another ?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>Yes, per assumption.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>What is the distribution?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>It depends on the KPI. </run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>Normal distribution: </run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Delivery Costs</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Delivery Distance</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Delivery Fee</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Fleet Delay</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- GFV</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Profit</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Revenue</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Travel Time</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Orders per Customer</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>Binomial distribution :</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Conversion Rate</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- mCVR2</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- mCVR3</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- mCVR4</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- User CVR</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Do the samples have equal variance ?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>Per assumption - no.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>How many samples are we comparing with one another ?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The answer varies from test to test. </run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The tests are divided in two groups : those with only 2 variants (regular A/B tests), and those with more than 2 variants (A/B/n tests).</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>Based on the answers to these questions, we can match our metrics with 3 different statistical methods :</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Chi square (binomial distribution with 2 variants)</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Chi square + Bonferroni correction (binomial distribution with &gt; 2 variants)</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Welch t-test (normal distribution with 2 variants)</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Welch ANOVA + Games Howell post-hoc test (normal distribution with &gt; 2 variants)</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Welch ANOVA + Games Howell - Two relevant P-Values</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The methodology of ANOVA with a post-hoc Games-Howell test requires looking at 2 p-values to decide if a result is significant.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>First, the ANOVA works on the test level (not between specific variants!) and asks - is there </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>any</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'> significant difference? </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>Only</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'> if there is a significant result, the Games-Howell test can tell us where (i.e. between which variables) the significant difference is.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>Therefore, if a Games-Howell test shows a significant result between two variables but the ANOVA test produced an insignificant result for the test - the result is insignificant.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The results of the ANOVA tests can be found in Significance Levels (advanced). For most users, it is recommended to </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>avoid</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'> using the advanced tab and base your analysis or the general tab.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>What is p-value?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>P-Value is the probability of making a type I error.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>What is alpha ?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>Alpha is the error rate allowed by the stakeholders. It is the upper threshold of acceptable p-value for the result to be considered statistically significant.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>What is the Bonferroni correction?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>The Bonferroni correction is a method of dealing with the multiple comparisons problem by adjusting the p-value to represent the larger probability of type I error when performing multiple comparisons. To control for this larger probability, we multiply the p-value by the number of comparisons.</run>
                  </formatted-text>
                  <zone-style>
                    <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                    <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                    <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                    <format attr='margin' value='4' />
                    <format attr='padding' value='0' />
                  </zone-style>
                </zone>
              </zone>
              <zone-style>
                <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                <format attr='margin' value='8' />
              </zone-style>
            </zone>
          </zones>
        </devicelayout>
      </devicelayouts>
      <simple-id uuid='{76E3E294-0B8E-4618-B0D0-FA5FE3FC5353}' />
    </dashboard>
    <dashboard _.fcp.AccessibleZoneTabOrder.true...enable-sort-zone-taborder='true' name='Significance Levels'>
      <repository-location derived-from='https://tableau.deliveryhero.net/t/GlobalStandardReporting/workbooks/DPSABTestSignificanceDashboard/SignificanceLevels?rev=' id='SignificanceLevels' path='/t/GlobalStandardReporting/workbooks/MockupDPSABTestSignificanceDashboard' revision='' site='GlobalStandardReporting' />
      <style>
        <style-rule element='dash-text'>
          <format attr='vertical-align' id='dash-text_186' value='top' />
          <format attr='vertical-align' id='dash-text_1578' value='top' />
        </style-rule>
        <style-rule element='parameter-ctrl'>
          <format attr='font-family' value='Tableau Medium' />
          <format attr='font-size' value='8' />
          <format attr='background-color' value='#00000000' />
        </style-rule>
        <style-rule element='parameter-ctrl-title'>
          <format attr='font-size' value='8' />
          <format attr='font-family' value='Tableau Medium' />
          <format attr='font-weight' value='normal' />
        </style-rule>
      </style>
      <size maxheight='1800' maxwidth='1600' minheight='1800' minwidth='1600' sizing-mode='fixed' />
      <datasources>
        <datasource name='Parameters' />
        <datasource caption='dps_ab_test_significance_dataset_v2' name='federated.1pu7wmd181yc111ags2hq081num9' />
      </datasources>
      <datasource-dependencies datasource='Parameters'>
        <column caption='Choose Alpha' datatype='real' name='[Parameter 4]' param-domain-type='range' role='measure' type='quantitative' value='0.050000000000000003'>
          <calculation class='tableau' formula='0.050000000000000003' />
          <range granularity='0.01' max='0.1' min='0.01' />
        </column>
        <column caption='Currency' datatype='string' name='[Parameter 5]' param-domain-type='list' role='measure' type='nominal' value='&quot;Euro&quot;'>
          <calculation class='tableau' formula='&quot;Euro&quot;' />
          <members>
            <member value='&quot;Euro&quot;' />
            <member value='&quot;Local&quot;' />
          </members>
        </column>
      </datasource-dependencies>
      <datasource-dependencies datasource='federated.1pu7wmd181yc111ags2hq081num9'>
        <column caption='KPI Label (Dynamic Definition)' datatype='string' name='[KPI Label (Aliases) (copy)_796503826216357888]' role='dimension' type='nominal'>
          <calculation class='tableau' formula='IF [kpi_label] = &quot;cvr&quot; THEN &quot;Conversion Rate&quot;&#10;ELSEIF [kpi_label] = &quot;mcvr2&quot; THEN &quot;mcvr2&quot;&#10;ELSEIF [kpi_label] = &quot;mcvr3&quot; THEN &quot;mcvr3&quot;&#10;ELSEIF [kpi_label] = &quot;mcvr4&quot; THEN &quot;mcvr4&quot;&#10;ELSEIF STARTSWITH([kpi_label], &quot;delivery_fee&quot;) THEN &quot;Delivery Fee&quot;&#10;ELSEIF STARTSWITH([kpi_label], &quot;gfv&quot;) THEN &quot;GFV&quot;&#10;ELSEIF [kpi_label] = &quot;travel_time&quot; THEN &quot;Travel Time&quot;&#10;ELSEIF STARTSWITH([kpi_label], &quot;delivery_costs&quot;) THEN &quot;Delivery Costs&quot;&#10;ELSEIF STARTSWITH([kpi_label], &quot;profit_eur&quot;) THEN &quot;Profit&quot;&#10;ELSEIF STARTSWITH([kpi_label], &quot;revenue_eur&quot;) THEN &quot;Revenue&quot;&#10;ELSEIF [kpi_label] = &quot;fleet_delay&quot; THEN &quot;Fleet Delay&quot;&#10;ELSEIF [kpi_label] = &quot;delivery_distance&quot; THEN &quot;Delivery Distance&quot;&#10;ELSEIF [kpi_label] = &quot;user_cvr&quot; THEN &quot;User CVR&quot;&#10;ELSEIF [kpi_label] = &quot;orders_per_customer&quot; THEN &quot;Orders per Customer&quot;&#10;ELSE &quot;Orders per User&quot;&#10;END' />
        </column>
        <column caption='Country Name' datatype='string' name='[country_name]' role='dimension' semantic-role='[Country].[ISO3166_2]' type='nominal' />
        <column-instance column='[KPI Label (Aliases) (copy)_796503826216357888]' derivation='None' name='[none:KPI Label (Aliases) (copy)_796503826216357888:nk]' pivot='key' type='nominal' />
        <column-instance column='[country_name]' derivation='None' name='[none:country_name:nk]' pivot='key' type='nominal' />
        <column-instance column='[test_name]' derivation='None' name='[none:test_name:nk]' pivot='key' type='nominal' />
        <column-instance column='[treatment]' derivation='None' name='[none:treatment:nk]' pivot='key' type='nominal' />
        <column-instance column='[variant_a]' derivation='None' name='[none:variant_a:nk]' pivot='key' type='nominal' />
        <column-instance column='[variant_b]' derivation='None' name='[none:variant_b:nk]' pivot='key' type='nominal' />
        <column caption='Test Name' datatype='string' name='[test_name]' role='dimension' type='nominal' />
        <column caption='Treatment' datatype='string' name='[treatment]' role='dimension' type='nominal' />
        <column caption='Variant A' datatype='string' name='[variant_a]' role='dimension' type='nominal' />
        <column caption='Variant B' datatype='string' name='[variant_b]' role='dimension' type='nominal' />
      </datasource-dependencies>
      <zones>
        <zone h='100000' id='4' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
          <zone h='99112' id='1194' param='horz' type-v2='layout-flow' w='99000' x='500' y='444'>
            <zone h='99112' id='323' type-v2='layout-basic' w='99000' x='500' y='444'>
              <zone h='99112' id='287' param='horz' type-v2='layout-flow' w='99000' x='500' y='444'>
                <zone h='99112' id='50' type-v2='layout-basic' w='99000' x='500' y='444'>
                  <zone forceUpdate='true' h='9500' id='11' type-v2='text' w='25500' x='23000' y='444'>
                    <formatted-text>
                      <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='20'>DPS A/B Test Dashboard</run>
                      <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                      <run bold='true' fontcolor='#898989' fontsize='16'>Significance Levels</run>
                    </formatted-text>
                    <zone-style>
                      <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                      <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                      <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                      <format attr='margin' value='4' />
                      <format attr='margin-top' value='30' />
                    </zone-style>
                  </zone>
                  <zone custom-title='true' h='3777' id='362' mode='list' param='[Parameters].[Parameter 5]' type-v2='paramctrl' w='7563' x='29125' y='12167'>
                    <formatted-text>
                      <run fontsize='8'>Currency</run>
                    </formatted-text>
                    <zone-style>
                      <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                      <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                      <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                      <format attr='margin' value='0' />
                      <format attr='background-color' value='#f5f5f5' />
                    </zone-style>
                  </zone>
                  <zone h='9500' id='494' is-centered='0' is-scaled='1' param='Image/DH Logistics Logo.png' type-v2='bitmap' w='22500' x='500' y='444'>
                    <zone-style>
                      <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                      <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                      <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                      <format attr='margin' value='4' />
                      <format attr='margin-top' value='30' />
                    </zone-style>
                  </zone>
                  <zone h='2223' id='528' name='Friendly Guide' show-title='false' w='17500' x='500' y='9944'>
                    <zone-style>
                      <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                      <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                      <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                      <format attr='margin' value='4' />
                    </zone-style>
                  </zone>
